          "text": "I now have everything I need:\n\n**1. visible_at_deadline** — Is an entry visible through membership at exactly the expiration time?\n\n`TTLCache.__contains__`:\n```python\n    def __contains__(self, key):\n        try:\n            link = self.__links[key]  # no reordering\n        except KeyError:\n            return False\n        else:\n            return self.timer() < link.expires\n```\n\nAt the exact expiration time, `self.timer() == link.expires`, so `self.timer() < link.expires` is `False`. The entry is NOT visible through membership. So visible_at_deadline = **false**.\n\nCitations: the `__contains__` body, specifically `return self.timer() < link.expires`. Maybe also `link.expires = time + self.__ttl` in `__setitem__` as the helper to justify that `link.expires` is the deadline. And the CHANGELOG note: \"TTLCache items now get expired if their expiration time is less...\" — I don't have the full line text; the search match shows truncated text \"...get expired if their expiration time is less\". I shouldn't quote that since it's truncated in the search result. I could read the CHANGELOG around offset 8071 to get the exact text, but I already have the operative statement in source. I'll use the source quotes.\n\n**2. expired_read_removes_storage** — Does an expired lookup physically remove its stored value?\n\n`TTLCache.__getitem__`:\n```python\n    def __getitem__(self, key, cache_getitem=Cache.__getitem__):\n        try:\n            link = self.__getlink(key)\n        except KeyError:\n            expired = False\n        else:\n            expired = not (self.timer() < link.expires)\n        if expired:\n            return self.__missing__(key)\n        else:\n            return cache_getitem(self, key)\n```\n\nIf expired, it calls `self.__missing__(key)` which, in `Cache`:\n```python\n    def __missing__(self, key):\n        raise KeyError(key)\n```\n\nThis just raises KeyError — it does not delete the value from `self.__data` (the underlying Cache storage). So the expired value physically remains in storage until something like `expire()` runs (which is triggered by `__len__`, `__repr__`, `__setitem__`, `popitem`, etc.). So expired_read_removes_storage = **false**.\n\nCitations: the `__getitem__` operative statement `return self.__missing__(key)` and `Cache.__missing__` raising KeyError.\n\n**3. assignment_refreshes_deadline** — Does assigning an existing live entry refresh its expiration?\n\n`TTLCache.__setitem__`:\n```python\n    def __setitem__(self, key, value, cache_setitem=Cache.__setitem__):\n        with self.timer as time:\n            self.expire(time)\n            cache_setitem(self, key, value)\n        try:\n            link = self.__getlink(key)\n        except KeyError:\n            self.__links[key] = link = TTLCache._Link(key)\n        else:\n            link.unlink()\n        link.expires = time + self.__ttl\n        link.next = root = self.__root\n        link.prev = prev = root.prev\n        prev.next = root.prev = link\n```\n\nYes — for an existing entry, the existing link is found and unlinked, then `link.expires = time + self.__ttl` re-sets expiration based on current frozen time. So assignment_refreshes_deadline = **true**.\n\nCitations: `link.expires = time + self.__ttl` and the `with self.timer as time:` line perhaps. The operative statement is `link.expires = time + self.__ttl`.\n\n**4. expire_reports_removed_pairs** — Does expire() report removed key/value pairs?\n\n```python\n    def expire(self, time=None):\n        \"\"\"Remove expired items from the cache and return an iterable of the\n        expired `(key, value)` pairs.\n\n        \"\"\"\n        if time is None:\n            time = self.timer()\n        root = self.__root\n        curr = root.next\n        links = self.__links\n        expired = []\n        cache_delitem = Cache.__delitem__\n        cache_getitem = Cache.__getitem__\n        while curr is not root and not (time < curr.expires):\n           